  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1252 PM MDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nUpper high pressure will continue to spin broadly over northern NM \nand the TX/OK Panhandle today through Thursday. Upper high begins \nshifting towards the Four Corners region Friday and throughout the \nweekend. Expect thunderstorm chances each afternoon with a focus \nover the area mountains (Gila region and Sacramento Mtns) and \nwestern locations bordering Arizona. Tuesday looks like it could \nhave an uptick in storm activity in southwestern NM Tuesday evening. \nCAMs are suggesting a large complex of storms in northwest Mexico \nwhich will push in better moisture to the southwestern portions of \nthe CWA later tonight. This push of moisture may help set the\nstage for Tuesday afternoon and evening. Looks like an area of\nsurface convergence is possible as well. Areas of flash flooding \nwill be possible. 0-6km shear values in this area will be around \n30-35KT which is sufficient for some organized cells. Wednesday \nand onward we keep our storm chances with a focus over the area \nmountains and western locations. \n\nAs far as temperatures go, the area will remain HOT the next 7 days. \nTemperatures will generally be 7-10 degrees above normal. A Heat \nAdvisory is in effect for several lowland locations including Las \nCruces, Hatch, the El Paso metro, and the lower Valley today and \ntomorrow. Triple digit readings will be common across the lowlands \nfor the week ahead. May need to extend the Heat Advisory potentially \nthrough Friday. High temperatures will be close or will reach Heat \nAdvisory criteria (max temperatures greater than or equal to 105F) \nduring this time. This will be an extended heat wave, so please take \nthe heat seriously.",
